D={
"Human–chimpanzee split":"Genetic comparisons show humans and chimpanzees share about 98.8% of their DNA and a common ancestor that lived roughly 6–8 million years ago in Africa. The split was messy: populations may have interbred for some time after separating. Fossils near this time — Sahelanthropus in Chad, Orrorin in Kenya — show early signs of upright walking, as forests broke into woodland mosaics.",
"First upright walking":"Bipedalism appeared before big brains or stone tools. Sahelanthropus (about 7 million years ago) had a skull opening suggesting upright posture, and Orrorin's thigh bones (6 million years ago) show walking. Theories for why include freeing hands to carry food, seeing over grass, keeping cool in the sun and moving efficiently between patches of forest. Lucy's species (3.2 million years ago) walked well but still climbed.",
"Laetoli footprints":"About 3.66 million years ago, at Laetoli in Tanzania, three individuals of Australopithecus afarensis walked across wet volcanic ash, which then hardened. Mary Leakey's team found the 27-metre trail in 1978. The footprints show a human-like arched foot and big toe in line with the others, proving fully upright walking long before stone tools.",
"First stone tools (Lomekwi)":"In 2015 archaeologists announced stone flakes and cores from Lomekwi 3 near Lake Turkana, Kenya, dated to 3.3 million years ago — 700,000 years older than the Oldowan and before the genus Homo. The makers (perhaps Kenyanthropus or Australopithecus) struck big rocks against anvils. Cut-marked bones from Dikika, Ethiopia (3.4 million years ago) suggest early meat-eating.",
"Oldowan toolmaking":"From about 2.6 million years ago at Gona, Ethiopia, hominins knapped simple sharp flakes from river cobbles to cut meat and crack bones for marrow. Named after Olduvai Gorge, Tanzania, the Oldowan is associated with Homo habilis and later early Homo erectus. Meat and marrow may have supplied energy for bigger brains.",
"Homo erectus leaves Africa":"By about 1.8 million years ago Homo erectus, taller and with longer legs, reached Dmanisi in Georgia, and soon Java and China. Stone tools in Shanghuang (China) and Attirampakkam (India, 1.5 million years ago) show how quickly hominins spread. Erectus survived in Java until about 110,000 years ago — the longest-lived human species.",
"Acheulean hand-axes":"About 1.76 million years ago in Kenya, Homo erectus began shaping symmetrical teardrop hand-axes — the first tools made to a mental template. The Acheulean tradition lasted over a million years from Africa to India (Attirampakkam, Bhimbetka) and Europe. Making them may have required planning and teaching, perhaps related to language origins.",
"Controlled fire at Wonderwerk Cave":"Burnt bone and plant ash deep inside Wonderwerk Cave in South Africa, about 1 million years ago, are the earliest secure evidence of hominins using fire. Fire allowed cooking, which makes food easier to digest; Richard Wrangham argues cooking drove brain growth and smaller guts. Fire also gave warmth, protection and social gatherings.",
"Regular hearths (Qesem Cave)":"At Qesem Cave in Israel, a hearth used repeatedly from about 300,000 years ago shows habitual fire use, with burnt bones from butchered fallow deer. Similar evidence appears at Gesher Benot Ya'aqov (780,000 years ago). Regular hearths show fire had become part of daily life.",
"Schöningen spears":"Wooden spears about 2 m long, found preserved in a German coal mine, date to about 300,000 years ago. Balanced like javelins, they were found with butchered horses, proving early humans (probably Homo heidelbergensis) hunted large animals rather than only scavenging.",
"Homo sapiens at Jebel Irhoud":"Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco, dated to about 315,000 years ago in 2017, are the oldest known Homo sapiens. They had modern faces but elongated braincases, suggesting our species emerged across Africa, not in one place.",
"Blombos Cave engravings":"In Blombos Cave, South Africa, people about 75,000–100,000 years ago engraved cross-hatched designs on ochre, made shell beads and mixed paint in abalone shells. These suggest symbolic thinking and, likely, language.",
"Arrival in Australia":"People reached Australia at least 50,000–65,000 years ago (Madjedbebe), crossing open sea from Southeast Asia. Aboriginal Australians have the oldest continuous cultures, with art, songlines and fire management.",
"Neanderthal–sapiens interbreeding":"Ancient DNA (Svante Pääbo, Nobel 2022) shows modern humans outside Africa carry 1–2% Neanderthal DNA from interbreeding about 50,000–60,000 years ago; Melanesians also carry Denisovan DNA. Some genes affect immunity and altitude adaptation (Tibetans).",
"Oldest figurative cave art (Sulawesi)":"A painting of pigs and human-like figures in Leang Karampuang, Sulawesi, is at least 51,200 years old — the oldest known figurative art, older than Europe's.",
"Neanderthal extinction":"Neanderthals disappeared around 40,000 years ago, after modern humans arrived in Europe. Causes include competition, small populations, climate swings and interbreeding.",
"Chauvet Cave paintings":"About 36,000 years ago, artists in southern France painted lions, horses, rhinos and mammoths with shading and perspective.",
"Dog domestication":"Dogs were domesticated from wolves at least 15,000 years ago, possibly earlier, by hunter-gatherers in Eurasia.",
"White Sands footprints":"Human footprints in New Mexico dated to 21,000–23,000 years ago suggest people were in the Americas during the Last Glacial Maximum.",
"Clovis culture":"Around 13,000 years ago Clovis hunters made fluted spear points across North America and hunted mammoths.",
"Natufian culture and first villages":"In the Levant from about 15,000 years ago, Natufian foragers built stone houses, harvested wild cereals with sickles and buried their dead — the first villages before farming. A 13,000-year-old brewery residue was found at Raqefet Cave.",
"Göbekli Tepe built":"Around 9600 BCE hunter-gatherers in southeast Turkey carved and raised T-shaped limestone pillars up to 5.5 m tall, decorated with foxes, snakes, vultures and scorpions, in circular enclosures. It is about 6,000 years older than Stonehenge and suggests monumental religion may have come before farming.",
"Jericho walls and tower":"Around 8000 BCE Jericho's farmers built a stone wall and an 8.5 m tower, perhaps against floods or enemies. Plastered skulls of ancestors show early ritual.",
"Rice cultivation in the Yangtze":"Rice was domesticated in the Yangtze valley around 9,000–7,000 years ago (Shangshan, Hemudu). Indian rice developed partly independently in the Ganga valley, with hybridisation later.",
"Maize domestication":"About 9,000 years ago farmers in Mexico's Balsas valley domesticated teosinte, a wild grass, into maize, which became the staple of the Americas.",
"Copper smelting":"From around 5000 BCE in Serbia (Belovode) and Iran, people smelted copper; bronze (copper and tin) followed by 3300 BCE.",
"Horse domestication":"Ancient DNA shows modern domestic horses came from the lower Volga–Don region about 2200 BCE; horses then spread rapidly with chariots.",
"Wheel and wagon":"The wheel appeared around 3500 BCE in Mesopotamia and Eastern Europe, first for pottery and then for wagons.",
"Narmer unifies Egypt":"Around 3100 BCE King Narmer united Upper and Lower Egypt, shown on the Narmer Palette.",
"Uruk, the first city":"By 3200 BCE Uruk in Sumer had perhaps 40,000 people, temples, and the first writing.",
"Great Pyramid built":"Built for Khufu about 2560 BCE, the Great Pyramid was 146 m tall with 2.3 million blocks — the tallest building for 3,800 years. Workers were paid labourers, as shown by workers' villages and the Wadi al-Jarf papyri.",
"Sargon founds Akkad":"About 2334 BCE Sargon conquered Sumer, creating the first empire.",
"Indus mature urban phase":"From 2600 to 1900 BCE, Harappa, Mohenjo-daro, Dholavira and Rakhigarhi had grid streets, drains and standard weights.",
"Code of Hammurabi":"Around 1754 BCE Hammurabi set up 282 laws on stone.",
"Thera eruption":"See Thera (Santorini) eruption: the Minoan island exploded around 1600 BCE.",
"Hyksos expelled from Egypt":"Ahmose I drove out the Hyksos about 1550 BCE, beginning the New Kingdom.",
"Akhenaten's religious revolution":"Akhenaten promoted worship of the Aten, the sun disc, and built Amarna.",
"Trojan War (traditional)":"Homer's war around 1200 BCE may reflect a real conflict at Troy (Hisarlik), destroyed around then.",
"Iron smelting spreads":"After 1200 BCE iron working spread across the Mediterranean, India (from about 1800 BCE in the Ganga valley and earlier in Tamil Nadu) and Africa.",
"Rigveda composed":"Between about 1500 and 1200 BCE, poets in the Punjab composed the Rigveda's hymns.",
}
